  "result": "Done. The worker added `split` to `shop/money.py` and a new test file, and the full suite passes.\n\n**What changed**\n\n- `shop/money.py`: new function `split(cents: int, parts: int) -> list[int]` at the end of the module. It uses `divmod` and gives the first `rem` elements one extra cent, so `split(10, 3)` returns `[4, 3, 3]`. A `parts` value below 1 raises `ValueError`, with a message in the same style as the existing `to_cents` error.\n- `tests/test_money.py`: new file, since money.py had no tests before. It uses `unittest` like the other test files. It covers an even split, remainder going to the first elements, `parts == 1`, a sum-and-length invariant over several inputs, and `ValueError` for `parts` of 0 and -1.\n\n**Test run**\n\nThe worker ran the command the README documents:\n\n```\npython3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t . -v\n```\n\nResult:\n\n```\nRan 11 tests in 0.001s\nOK\n```\n\nNothing was committed. One note: `split` with negative `cents` follows Python floor division, so `split(-7, 3)` gives `[-2, -2, -3]`, which still sums correctly. The spec did not cover negatives, so no guard was added.",
